This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we derive a critical growth condition for $\mu$ as $mu'<0$. Furthermore, we prove the existence of a normalized ground state solution using a monotonicity argument and the constrained local minimum theorem, and this solution possesses symmetry properties due to the translation symmetry of the equation. A key step involves the establishment of a monotonicity property in a modified Orlicz space. Additionally, by taking advantage of the critical exponent $\mu'$ and growth conditions of $f$, we can obtain a priori bounds for the normalized ground state solution, and this allows us to prove the existence of a localized ground state solution within a subspace with compact support. Our method is based on the modified Orlicz space and the properties of the Riesz transforms, and we employ a variety of mathematical techniques including the monotonicity argument, the constrained local minimum theorem, and the properties of the ground state solutions. The results obtained in this paper can be extended and generalized to other types of nonlinear elliptic equations, including the Choquard equation, the Yang-Mills equation, and the Yang-Mills-Higgs model, under both local and nonlocal interactions. The proof of the monotonicity property in Theorem 3.1 is the key to the entire proof of this paper, and it is based on the properties of the Riesz transforms and the Lorentz spaces. The details of the proof can be found in the given reference. Due to the space limitation, we have omitted the detailed proof of many important lemmas and theorems, and we refer the readers to the given reference for more information. The main ideas and methods used in this paper are

A prominent paradigm in reward learning utilizes human feedback via comparative evaluations, but their broader adoption is hindered by the expense and labor intensity of these comparisons. To overcome this limitation, we propose grouping multiple comparisons together and allowing human labelers to mark sub-regions of a condensed state space representation, thereby efficiently leveraging human time. Through data visualization techniques, we create an interactive interface for users to label these regions, thereby effectively enhancing the learning agents' performance, even when human labeling time remains constant, across basic Mujoco tasks. Crucially, our proposed method enables time-efficient reward learning for complex real-world tasks without requiring significant setup time. We demonstrate improved learning outcomes in tasks such as navigation and reward-based manipulation, outperforming a comparison method that uses individual comparisons. Additionally, we show our approach can be extended to learn more nuanced rewards. Our code and data are publicly available for reproduction of our results. 

