Our team has developed innovative photonic solutions using astrophotonic chips, 
which enable the production of multiple dispersion spectra across various wavelengths.
These innovative devices are now being used to enhance the resolution of spectrographic 
instruments on a global scale, spanning from Earth's lab to space-based observatories.
We will introduce the concept of astrophotonic chips and optimize their applications, 
particularly in the domain of ordinary white light, where our design outperforms conventional 
diffraction gratings by an order of magnitude.
Furthermore, our research demonstrates that replacing a conventional slits device with an astrophotonic 
chip within a multi-object spectrograph yields superior results, outperforming other alternatives.
Lastly, we will highlight the exceptional resilience of these devices to temperature fluctuations, 
making them ideal for use in space-based observatories. This research has far-reaching implications 
for a range of applications, from astronomy to laboratory settings, allowing users to access a 
wealth of unprecedented resolution and precision in their data collection processes. 

